#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 720.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199077} :activity/id #uuid "da7c19c9-a092-5227-8c3d-c60c670a8222" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048206 :topic/id #uuid "8850e3a0-f2fb-5671-843a-13422a22aa79" :topic/math-academy-id 4387} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199077 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199560 :tutorial/content "Recall that if $A$ and $B$ are sets, the Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs $(a,b)$ whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA \\times B = \\big\\{(a, b) \\: : \\: a \\in A,\\, b \\in B \\big\\}\n$$\n\nMany Cartesian products have natural representations in the Cartesian plane. The most common of these is the Cartesian product $\\mathbb R^2{:}$\n\n$$\n\\mathbb R^2 = \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R =  \\big\\{(x, y) \\: : \\: x,y \\in \\mathbb R \\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis infinite set contains all ordered pairs $(x,y),$ where $x$ and $y$ can be any real numbers. In other words, $\\mathbb R^2$ contains every point in the Cartesian plane! \n\n![](images/07/070389fcdb6d69f9aa90dabbd98a0d3461e26d3e3e08b1d53b92e99688c09923.png)\n\nSimilarly, the set\n\n$$\n\\mathbb R^3 = \\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R\\times\\mathbb R =  \\big\\{(x, y, z) \\: : \\: x,y,z \\in \\mathbb R \\big\\}\n$$\n\ndescribes every point in three-dimensional Cartesian space. \n\n![](images/e7/e74fb5948144bc61173e4de73865cb746278bd31fd87e4abf927fa01821ba936.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "c18e6618-573b-5560-8d95-58ed56f05155" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8237 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "c7652427-e5ff-5d02-9f23-39039f090aff" :step/math-academy-id 153383 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199078}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199078 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198272 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198169 :question/id #uuid "bb40c174-3739-50df-835e-5d47c9902d5b" :question/math-academy-id "e-13192"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c3a2b291-c6dd-58c8-a10f-d851511d7bfc" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048899} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050432} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050763} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050923}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198807} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287775} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287870} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287871} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389895} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390326} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421830}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "2190f1df-5853-5d8c-85e2-33f40f1f0f24" :step/math-academy-id 153384 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199079}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199079 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199579 :tutorial/content "Cartesian coordinates can also be used to visualize other Cartesian products.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following Cartesian product:\n\n$$\n\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^- = \\big\\{(x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in\\mathbb N,\\, y\\in\\mathbb R^-\\big\\}\n$$\n\nThis product contains every point in $\\mathbb R^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is a natural number and whose $y$-coordinate is a negative real number.\n\nTo visualize this Cartesian product, consider the following subset of our Cartesian product:\n\n$$\nS_1 = \\big\\{ (1,y) \\: : \\: y \\in\\mathbb{R}^- \\big\\} \\subset \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-\n$$\n\nThis subset describes all points in the plane whose $x$-coordinate equals $1$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative real number. We can represent $S_1$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/f6/f6165f66b4149b7b2ea52a3cd686836853d85048ca890e850cb771174a150c57.png)\n\nNext, we consider the subset $S_2,$ given by\n\n$$\nS_2 = \\big\\{ (2,y) \\: : \\: y \\in\\mathbb{R}^- \\big\\} \\subset \\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-.\n$$\n\nThis subset describes all points in the plane whose $x$-coordinate equals $2$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any negative real number. \n\n![](images/ca/caf2299a2e8009ad5b2776ce67786fa7c7e3bdb57aacbd7df87882f59a2350e2.png)\n\nWe can create the subsets $S_3, S_4, S_5,\\ldots,$ similarly. Our Cartesian product consists of the *union* of these subsets:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N\\times {R}^{-} & ={S}_{1}\\cup {S}_{2}\\cup {S}_{3}\\cup ⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can visualize $\\mathbb{N} \\times \\mathbb{R}^-$ as the set of vertical rays shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/03/033c40b86bfd5cebf24d34de644f632213207386c2b67c559af7594468599e1e.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1626e562-8e88-5ff3-bae3-4162438c0086" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8329 :tutorial/title "Cartesian Products of Countably Infinite Sets"} :step/id #uuid "cd371945-c79b-5b78-9017-15c39e4d5ed7" :step/math-academy-id 153387 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199080}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199080 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198273 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198154 :question/id #uuid "eae94514-cd92-5405-90b1-ee09dad18e13" :question/math-academy-id "e-13162"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e2aee319-68d6-54f7-8023-b24d32610bc4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050088}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198778} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198779} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287749} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287752} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287753} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287777} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287784} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287787} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389909} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389916}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching Cartesian Products of Special Sets in the Coordinate Plane"} :step/id #uuid "48b1fb46-7071-5897-9dd7-a47e1d955f6c" :step/math-academy-id 153388 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199081}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199081 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199584 :tutorial/content "Let's sketch a representation of the set $S= [0,1] \\times [0,1]$ in the Cartesian plane.\n\nThe Cartesian product $A \\times B$ consists of all ordered pairs whose first coordinate belongs to $A$ and whose second coordinate belongs to $B.$\n\n$$\nA\\times B = \\big\\{(a,b) \\::\\: a\\in A,\\, b\\in B\\big\\}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the set\n\n$$\nS = [0,1] \\times [0, 1]\n$$\n\ncontains every point in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ whose $x$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS=\n\\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: x\\in [0,1],\\, y\\in [0,1] \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nTo visualize $S$, consider $S_0 \\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals ${\\color{red}0}$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS_{\\color{red}0} = \\big\\{ ({\\color{red}{0}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in [0,1]\\big\\}\n$$\n\nWe can represent $S_{\\color{red}0}$ in the plane as follows:\n\n![](images/1c/1cc30fcc7543bfbbf8c5c59d1436eb3b1a0799d60c9ad1d899957e8fd17d58ab.png)\n\nNow, consider a general $S_{\\color{blue}{i}} \\subset S$ containing all points whose $x$-coordinate equals some ${\\color{blue}{i}} \\in [0,1]$ and whose $y$-coordinate is any real number in the interval $[0,1].$\n\n$$\nS_{\\color{blue}{i}} = \\big\\{ ({\\color{blue}{i}},y) \\: : \\: y \\in [0,1]\\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor each ${\\color{blue}{i}} \\in [0,1],$ $S_{\\color{blue}{i}}$ is a vertical line segment from the point $({\\color{blue}{i}},0)$ to the point $({\\color{blue}{i}},1).$\n\n![](images/28/28b9aa0559635ed4090f10dd0b59f2cbb6c297f8736d9509a637b5d78d27561c.png)\n\nOur Cartesian product $S$ is the union of these subsets over $I = [0,1]{:}$\n\n$$\nS = \\bigcup_{{\\color{blue}i} \\in I} S_{\\color{blue}i}.\n$$\n\nAs $\\color{blue}i$ runs over $I,$ these line segments sweep the following rectangle that represents the union $\\displaystyle \\bigcup_{{\\color{blue}i} \\in I} S_{\\color{blue}i}{:}$\n\n![](images/76/76ff48755dbfc3ba1943665d08aa8cfd00948bbc1ddfaab560a0af64f11bae26.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "923fb377-d694-5181-8561-c57612f15054" :tutorial/math-academy-id 8339 :tutorial/title "The Cartesian Product [0,1] x [0,1]"} :step/id #uuid "ef01039c-9cce-558a-8067-2ffba087e848" :step/math-academy-id 153392 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199082}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198274 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198205 :question/id #uuid "aa337681-5d8c-5ea1-b0fe-5d8cd15040a7" :question/math-academy-id "e-13320"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6290e701-b4ea-5a86-ab7e-7371c5bdc11e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198716} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198806} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287751} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287873} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287874} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389804} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389811} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389923} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389930} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186390333}] :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Unions of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "9ddfd97d-7a1a-59ef-8492-3fbe4088f22f" :step/math-academy-id 153393 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186199083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198275 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198206 :question/id #uuid "67569060-2f33-5a8d-ae73-690742d3d318" :question/math-academy-id "e-13321"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5793f07d-e0de-5707-a5e1-32d657244e0b" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198717} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198719} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284730} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284731} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284732} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284750} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287875} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287876} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186287877} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186367928} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186389797}] :knowledge-point/title "Visualizing Intersections of Uncountable Families of Indexed Sets"} :step/id #uuid "5777ec1b-f050-570f-86e4-1e7bffa77dfb" :step/math-academy-id 153396}] :activity/title "Visualizing Cartesian Products" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186198532}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162990} :activity/id #uuid "2698ccf6-18f8-573f-9adf-9c8bdcc0f6e7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048325 :topic/id #uuid "95207fcf-4f1f-5c3f-a008-9c15baaebacc" :topic/math-academy-id 3002} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162990 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163281 :tutorial/content "The joint PMF $f(x,y)$ of two discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ tells us all possible events $(X, Y)$ and the probabilities associated with each event. However, suppose we wish to determine the probability mass function for $X$ only. How can this be deduced from the joint PMF?\n\nLet's build some intuition by considering an example.\n\nConsider the joint probability mass function $f(x,y)$ for the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y,$ shown below.\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X={\\color{blue}{1}}$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X={\\color{red}{2}}$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ |\n\nThe support of $X$ is $S_X = \\{{\\color{blue}{1}},{\\color{red}{2}}\\}.$ Let's now deduce the probabilities associated with each value of $X$ in $S_X\\mathbin{:}$\n\n- According to the law of total probability, we can deduce $P(X=1)$ by summing the values in the first row of the table, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=1) & =P(X=1,Y=1)+P(X=1,Y=2) \\\\  & =0.05+0.15 \\\\  & =0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we summed over all possible values of $Y$ associated with $X=1.$\n\n- Similarly, by summing the values in the second row in the table, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=2) & =P(X=2,Y=1)+P(X=2,Y=2) \\\\  & =0.7+0.1 \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we summed over all possible values of $Y$ associated with $X=2.$\n\nTherefore, the probability mass function for $X$ is as follows:\n\n| $x$ | $\\color{blue}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_X(x)$ | $0.2$ | $0.8$ |\n\nNotice that we have used the notation $f_X(x).$ Since $f_X(x)$ was deduced from the joint probability mass function $f(x,y),$ we call $f_X(x)$ the **marginal mass function** for $X.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "0281a245-2f6d-5a89-8ea7-5d77bf3dca4f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5469 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "0dce6d66-b5fe-52cc-ae58-8918d96eb144" :step/math-academy-id 34052 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163082}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162997 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162071 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161871 :question/id #uuid "da693f0e-db20-5a7f-9737-3f26d5d6581c" :question/math-academy-id "e-9189"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96760d15-5e56-529b-a5da-1697dc782031" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051001}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272356} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272357} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272358} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272359}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal Probability"} :step/id #uuid "b36fe44c-1d35-5f90-8f95-9d5746329173" :step/math-academy-id 34274 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163063}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162072 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161872 :question/id #uuid "e99dc081-a5d6-5770-a0ea-da172b0d37f5" :question/math-academy-id "e-9190"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "96473ec4-67f8-5614-b187-0961062aac29" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162375} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272361} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272363} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272364} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272365}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Cumulative Probability Given a Joint Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "6e2655db-a7c3-5298-b5ab-6b47cfe4c0e4" :step/math-academy-id 34277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163063 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162102 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161668 :question/id #uuid "162ebeba-e9ee-5588-8d79-659754a77548" :question/math-academy-id "e-10044"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ac80eed8-d801-5275-9bd1-73ba43fadb38" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162369} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272372} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272374} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272377} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272379}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Marginal Probability Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "f514a8f7-d466-5f40-8ce4-7fcfebb6e28c" :step/math-academy-id 37907 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162998}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163082 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163331 :tutorial/content "Let's go back to the last example for the random variables $X$ and $Y.$\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ |\n\nWe saw that the marginal mass function $f_X$ for $X$ is found by summing the rows in the table. So let's add the sum of each row to our table:\n\n| $f$ | $Y=1$ | $Y=2$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.2}$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.8}$ |\n\nNotice how we wrote the values of the *marginal* mass function in the right *margin* of the table.\n\nFrom here, we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\nP(X=1) = f_X(1) = 0.2, \\qquad P(X=2) = f_X(2) = 0.8.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, we can compute the marginal mass function of $Y,$ denoted $f_Y(y),$ by summing the columns. Adding these totals to our table, we get the following:\n\n| $f$ | $Y={\\color{blue}{1}}$ | $Y={\\color{red}{2}}$ | $f_X$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $X=1$ | $0.05$ | $0.15$ | $0.2$ |\n| $X=2$ | $0.7$ | $0.1$ | $0.8$ |\n| $f_Y$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.75}$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.25}$ | $1$ |\n\nAgain, notice how we wrote the values of the *marginal* mass function in the bottom *margin* of the table.\n\nFrom here, we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\nP(Y={\\color{blue}{1}}) = f_Y({\\color{blue}{1}}) = 0.75, \\qquad P(Y={\\color{red}{2}}) = f_Y({\\color{red}{2}}) = 0.25.\n$$\n\nFinally, the marginal mass function $f_Y(y)$ of $Y$ is as follows:\n\n| $y$ | $\\color{blue}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f_Y(y)$ | $0.75$ | $0.25$ |" :tutorial/id #uuid "a1c9ecf8-619b-58b7-899f-b179dca8ae45" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6290 :tutorial/title "Deducing the Marginal Mass Function for Y"} :step/id #uuid "83e1320a-f77b-5173-bedf-f02c9d400247" :step/math-academy-id 38105 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163083}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163083 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163332 :tutorial/content "Now that we've built some intuition, let's define the marginal mass functions formally.\n\nLet $f(x,y)$ be the joint PMF of the discrete random variables $X$ and $Y$ with supports $S_X$ and $S_Y$, respectively.\n\n- The **marginal mass function ** of $X,$ denoted $f_X(x),$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_X(x) = P(X=x)  = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{y \\in S_Y} f(x,y).\n$$\n\n- The **marginal mass function ** of $Y,$ denoted $f_Y(y),$ is defined as\n\n$$\nf_Y(y)= P(Y=y)  = \\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S_X} f(x,y).\n$$\n\nWe can relate these definitions to our previous intuition:\n\n- The first definition states that, to compute $P(X=x)$ for some particular value $x,$ we sum all possible values of $Y$ in the row corresponding to $X=x.$\n\n- The second definition states that, to compute $P(Y=y)$ for some particular value $y,$ we sum all possible values of $X$ in the column corresponding to $Y=y.$\n\nFinally, note that marginal mass functions are sometimes called **marginal probability mass functions**, **marginal PMFs**, or (less formally) **marginal distributions**." :tutorial/id #uuid "ddca7e0f-a315-5567-97d7-f5b40876617b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6291 :tutorial/title "A Formal Definition of the Marginal Mass Function"} :step/id #uuid "97117126-1ac4-57aa-9c23-c3e882c06372" :step/math-academy-id 38106 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162997}}] :activity/title "Marginal Distributions for Discrete Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162163}] [{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193613} :activity/id #uuid "e3d33137-c31a-567d-baff-3e933e4f2403" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048532 :topic/id #uuid "a7907bdb-038e-5baa-a4ae-df7b362e291b" :topic/math-academy-id 4100} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193612 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192754 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192473 :question/id #uuid "3f141210-8194-5346-9e2a-c47f98bb3443" :question/math-academy-id "e-10840"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1fdf4f96-f679-57b8-96aa-7e9c34b986b8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048744}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193390} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193392} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262576} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262577} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262578} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262579} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354555} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421865} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421866}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a Probability Over an Unbounded Interval"} :step/id #uuid "d9a24908-03a6-51d6-b5f1-3155cc2dccaf" :step/math-academy-id 41081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193613 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193862 :tutorial/content "Recall that the **probability density function** (or **pdf**) of a continuous random variable $X$ defined over a set $S$ is a function $f(x)$ that satisfies the following conditions:\n\n- $f(x) \\geq 0$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n- $\\displaystyle \\int_{S} f(x) \\, \\textrm dx = 1$\n\nIt is common for the set $S$ of a continuous random variable $X$ to be an infinite interval. Therefore, we often need to evaluate improper integrals when determining whether a function is a valid pdf and to compute probabilities associated with such a random variable.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following function:\n\n![](images/38/38ea105f5dd0cea7784bf73f814e7161b90cfd224eb099084cc33b6539d95fdd.svg)\n\nIs $f(x)$ a valid probability density function? Let's start by sketching its graph:\n\n![](images/35/3559a998491a004c159683aaf0946bb4fdf87f4afe873fdc93235e840b4b0ac8.png)\n\nClearly, $f(x)\\geq 0$ for all $x,$ so this function satisfies the first condition.\n\nTo check that $f(x)$ satisfies the second condition, we need to compute the following integral:\n\n$$\n\\int_S f(x)\\,\\textrm d x = \\int_0^\\infty e^{-x}\\,\\textrm d x\n$$\n\nCalculating this integral using the usual methods, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-x}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[-{e}^{-x}]}_{0}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =[-{e}^{-\\infty }]-[-{e}^{0}] \\\\  & =0-(-1) \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since $f(x)$ satisfies both conditions, we conclude that $f(x)$ is a valid probability density function.\n\nLet's see another example." :tutorial/id #uuid "f9c0835e-afeb-5f5c-b296-1e37ad68d3fa" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6775 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "00b1a7d3-74fb-5434-befa-306fc78d3a8a" :step/math-academy-id 41119 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193693}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193614 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192755 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192474 :question/id #uuid "d40a67bd-7d98-58fb-b4a1-7a556e91556a" :question/math-academy-id "e-10851"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fd8455e1-46f3-5956-80ec-e6753cc005a7" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193391} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193394} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278307} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278308} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278309} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278310} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278311} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354558} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354559} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408037}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Constant Given a PDF"} :step/id #uuid "cccafa59-dc36-5d28-96de-c705cc5a1797" :step/math-academy-id 41121 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193612}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193693 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192801 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192529 :question/id #uuid "5f988175-cdd9-5ddf-94b5-3b995dc75fce" :question/math-academy-id "e-12329"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "441a1de0-dc64-59fe-99a8-0ab502841425" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048115} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049638} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051015}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193133} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193204} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278302} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278304} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278305} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408038}] :knowledge-point/title "Checking Whether a Given Function Is a Valid PDF"} :step/id #uuid "369bab2a-0778-58cb-b362-2b26468c8bd0" :step/math-academy-id 44173 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186193614}}] :activity/title "Continuous Random Variables Over Infinite Domains" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186192925}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421869} :activity/id #uuid "2dc4dabc-aedf-5892-921f-490e984369c4" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048532 :topic/id #uuid "a7907bdb-038e-5baa-a4ae-df7b362e291b" :topic/math-academy-id 4100} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421867 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6023af0f-b4dd-541f-b0d2-9db3f518cd70" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260707} {:answer/id #uuid "d02f4e02-7b6c-5777-aee2-33c54a892f35" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261714} {:answer/id #uuid "d6172bf6-5ae9-5323-b13a-c469aa93b4d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261758} {:answer/id #uuid "ea772249-1b9e-54d7-aff3-07375a0afdd3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261933} {:answer/id #uuid "f5989cb2-1e59-5e2c-9106-aaed97e1d35e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262023}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260707} :answer-field/id #uuid "249bf36d-1590-59fa-bf5d-ae59c8724944" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262188}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4715a163-98e1-5514-9f6b-3d69d82eb15c" :question/math-academy-id "q-126028" :question/problem "Compute $P(X>4)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1, \\\\ \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}},\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>4) & =P(4<X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}]}_{4}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "01b175bd-e667-5a75-b3df-02b40ee2e81b"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421868 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421866 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28308daa-8ddb-545e-8465-8f0a73db0af6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421855} {:answer/id #uuid "8e8aadca-e9d8-5956-bfd5-24321e9b6037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{27}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421857} {:answer/id #uuid "a495ea88-69a7-52f5-b2d7-6986e9a706f6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{64}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421858} {:answer/id #uuid "c724da1c-2c42-5050-aba4-21fa7939277a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{81}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421859} {:answer/id #uuid "e8dbe1c3-a9e4-5566-b39f-c692e5091488" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421861}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421855} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d3bf738-9bc7-5e68-a507-db9436929f3e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "942a9198-9a9b-5910-a327-96717799bf91" :question/math-academy-id "q-87226" :question/problem "Compute $P(X>3)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}},\\, & x\\ge 1, \\\\ 0,\\, & x<1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>3) & =P(3\\le X<\\infty ) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{\\infty }\\frac{4}{{x}^{5}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{{x}^{4}}]}_{3}^{\\infty } \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{81}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "1be2574c-a3ce-5ec0-bb9f-88e4dc8755a2" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421867}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421869 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421865 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "146aee1a-528b-5bbf-a4b6-4fbf2f271f49" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-20}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421853} {:answer/id #uuid "27715182-2cb9-52c2-a080-36df60c28e78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421854} {:answer/id #uuid "7e007eff-0deb-57f5-a6ba-37ea62edbf05" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421856} {:answer/id #uuid "e008e939-3bb6-57fd-a8f3-41e658e78a3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-4/5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421860} {:answer/id #uuid "f5c19772-5317-5a44-8357-af32f4360941" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{e}^{-10}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421862}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421853} :answer-field/id #uuid "55603476-e287-5779-991f-12f57c478278" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "168818a7-6140-5751-9382-8a48afe2a16e" :question/math-academy-id "q-87221" :question/problem "Compute $P(X<-5)$ given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}4{e}^{4x},\\, & x\\le 0, \\\\ 0,\\, & x>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "If a continuous random variable $X$ has the probability density function $f(x),$ then \n\n$$\nP(a<X<b)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, in our case, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<-5) & =P(-\\infty <X<-5) \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-5}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{-5}4{e}^{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{e}^{4x}]}_{-\\infty }^{-5} \\\\  & ={e}^{-20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "fe10f7a6-87e1-5126-8846-1670d19ab5f3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186421868}}] :activity/title "Continuous Random Variables Over Infinite Domains" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186421852}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176605} :activity/id #uuid "cac9bb11-d48e-5140-9653-cc117881f57c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048748 :topic/id #uuid "bad1cfe2-7639-5714-8417-2b8af7ece5cb" :topic/math-academy-id 3571} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176605 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176968 :tutorial/content "Under what conditions can we be sure that the global extrema of a function exist?\n\nThe **extreme value theorem** answers this question and states the following:\n\n> *If $f(x)$ is a continuous function defined on the closed interval $[a,b],$ then $f(x)$ always has at least one global maximum and one global minimum on $[a,b]$*.\n\nFor example, consider the function $f(x),$ given by\n\n$$\nf(x) = (x-3)^2, \\qquad x\\in [2,5].\n$$\n\nNotice that this function satisfies the conditions for the extreme value theorem:\n\n- the function $f(x)$ is *continuous* on the interval $x\\in [2,5]$, and\n\n- the interval $[2,5]$ is *closed*.\n\nSince *both* conditions are satisfied, the extreme value theorem guarantees that $f(x)$ must have at least one global maximum and one global minimum.\n\nTo see that this is indeed true, let's sketch the graph of $f(x).$ \n\n![](images/d2/d2ceffc40ead80b03d73004b663b91a942444bd14ce9e1e68fc98ffebbae3423.png)\n\nFrom the diagram, we see that the function $f(x)$ has \n\n- a global maximum at $x=5,$ and\n\n- a global minimum at $x=3.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "300a3bb8-a79f-5387-8c5e-1e403c2df531" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2666 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9ff3fa5a-39c6-574d-bfa0-7e467bf44083" :step/math-academy-id 12791 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176805}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176606 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175901 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175768 :question/id #uuid "59426214-0bb1-5e8b-9e96-0fb84ec6361c" :question/math-academy-id "e-3732"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "eede91c4-9099-55df-a803-f87895d9f51b" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049593}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176497} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186234352} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266042} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266043} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266044} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266059} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341990} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341993} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341994} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186342003}] :knowledge-point/title "Applying the Extreme Value Theorem to Piecewise Functions"} :step/id #uuid "8c5c661d-87d1-5fdd-b4cf-9b1217993d69" :step/math-academy-id 12792} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176607 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175902 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175769 :question/id #uuid "2d31d7a1-bd6a-5794-a4af-b6cdad4dc64d" :question/math-academy-id "e-3733"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "205c5518-c67a-5784-8cc7-55b42ce7fb0e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049162} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050574}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176494} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262692} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262693} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341975} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341981}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether the Extreme Value Theorem Is Applicable to a Function Given Its Graph"} :step/id #uuid "3001b2d1-c302-5b15-b3a6-3ff0fa36821e" :step/math-academy-id 12794 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176608}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176608 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175903 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175770 :question/id #uuid "1d90dec2-3c79-5069-ba1b-7e7e3f16ddb9" :question/math-academy-id "e-3735"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bbc6a2d-2078-50d5-9584-c705c19212da" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051076}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176496} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176498} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262966} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262967} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262992} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329851} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186329852} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186341988}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Intervals on Which a Given Function Satisfies the Extreme Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "7889a404-286a-5541-b477-ed0966b909de" :step/math-academy-id 12795 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176606}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176805 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177036 :tutorial/content "We can only apply the extreme value theorem to *continuous* functions defined on *closed* intervals. Both conditions are crucial.\n\nTo see why this is the case, consider the function $g(x),$ given by\n\n$$\ng(x)=(x-3)^2, \\qquad x\\in [2,5).\n$$\n\nNotice that $g(x)$ is defined on a semi-open interval (the right endpoint is open). As a result, we cannot guarantee that $g(x)$ has a global maximum and a global minimum.\n\n![](images/a7/a7648d89fc02372f8d48a649425fa5dba4c8a86e12e9fa7bbddc69ffe52575c3.png)\n\nSimilarly, consider the function $h(x),$ given by\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}, & \\,2\\le x<3, \\\\ (x-3)^{2}+1, & \\,3\\le x\\le 5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $h(x)$ is defined on the closed interval $[2,5].$ However, since $h(x)$ is not continuous on $[2,5],$ we cannot guarantee that $h(x)$ has both a global maximum and minimum on $[2,5].$\n\n![](images/b1/b1bdc0a3c384e5918bdac46ce5db31c0836069eb8a3383febf2aac86654929ea.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "08bf8574-c1f7-5521-983b-282f7b0c3cc3" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5745 :tutorial/title "Conditions for Applying the Extreme Value Theorem"} :step/id #uuid "fa2543e5-fe3a-57fe-a964-118b017f51bc" :step/math-academy-id 35242 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176607}}] :activity/title "The Extreme Value Theorem" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176121}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176678} :activity/id #uuid "6410482c-1a4b-55b8-afc6-f009af1b05ff" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048973 :topic/id #uuid "d050a7cc-fb5e-58e5-a1a7-41ca68d4da28" :topic/math-academy-id 3574} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175928 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175780 :question/id #uuid "e65b269e-a33e-5bc6-b13a-c3f12488f260" :question/math-academy-id "e-5527"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "00cf6180-070b-5ebf-af24-bc4c4b0bd94e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048775}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176478} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176481} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272074} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272075} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326678} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351199} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351201} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351202} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351251} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351252} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186394119}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvector of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvalue"} :step/id #uuid "c6666ad3-c461-5111-a9f4-d5a6f4f8e51a" :step/math-academy-id 20163 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176645}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176644 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175929 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175781 :question/id #uuid "acaa8e8f-de36-56b0-8e02-ded7fd08118c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5528"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d0e83dcf-3ce6-5583-bd28-fdbc805a9403" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048819} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048861}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176477} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176575} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272072} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272073} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326677} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351197} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351198} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411584}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding a Complex Eigenvalue of a Matrix Given a Complex Eigenvector"} :step/id #uuid "3ddd6359-72c0-5eee-87d9-df029bef4a21" :step/math-academy-id 20164 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176643}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176645 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175930 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175782 :question/id #uuid "da3e8948-1f17-5281-a2f7-2fb1732939d9" :question/math-academy-id "e-5529"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4ad17d11-325f-5888-a5cd-61ee27f7a103" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048305}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176479} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176480} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272076} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272077} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326684} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326746} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326747} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326748} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351254} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375432}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Two Linearly Independent Complex Eigenvectors of a 2x2 Matrix"} :step/id #uuid "8cbda370-20f2-5e6b-9780-b0122e4ee2a0" :step/math-academy-id 20166} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176678 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176975 :tutorial/content "Let's find the eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=-\\textrm{i}$ of the matrix $[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A - \\lambda I) \\textbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =-\\textrm{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1+\\text{i} & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(1+\\text{i}){x}_{1}-{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+(-1+\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(-\\textrm{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another.\n\nAs a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\nx_2 = (1+\\textrm{i})x_1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by setting $x_1\\in\\mathbb C$ to be the free variable, we find that the general solution to our system is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ (1+\\text{i}){x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSetting $x_1=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that our eigenvector has complex-valued entries.\n\n**Note:** To check our result, we can show that $A\\mathbf v = \\lambda \\mathbf v.$ Computing $A\\mathbf v,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-\\text{i} \\\\ 1-\\text{i}\\end{aligned}],\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand computing $\\lambda \\mathbf v$ gives\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1+\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nSo $A\\mathbf v = \\lambda \\mathbf v,$ as required." :tutorial/id #uuid "1bde3a85-d8df-5194-8581-77fe2108627c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4297 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8f4ddd6c-a17e-54f3-bade-a64685e04b36" :step/math-academy-id 26123 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176644}}] :activity/title "Finding Complex Eigenvectors of Real 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176123}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411587} :activity/id #uuid "3099b967-b00c-5782-9a83-fd84f9ad579c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048973 :topic/id #uuid "d050a7cc-fb5e-58e5-a1a7-41ca68d4da28" :topic/math-academy-id 3574} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411585 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351252 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0489168e-93f4-5458-8a02-7bdcd1fa6253" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348487} {:answer/id #uuid "4f637c61-750b-5562-b8aa-12c7f8e8f1af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349164} {:answer/id #uuid "733e337e-a48f-5c12-902f-9d1678192174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1+\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349506} {:answer/id #uuid "dc6761e2-57cb-5fcc-9965-269be85a60a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350374} {:answer/id #uuid "f3b3ce77-31f6-5c5b-9f4d-8e85c892abd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350580}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186350374} :answer-field/id #uuid "674d76e0-0dad-52e1-9b92-041ef1633c77" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350876}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fbfe9a5f-3d3d-54aa-9e82-2b9e587c84ba" :question/math-academy-id "q-81185" :question/problem "If $v=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of \n$A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]$\nthat corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda =2-3\\text{i},$\nthen what is the value of $\\frac{a}{b}?$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n\n$$\n(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn our case, we have $\\,\\lambda =2-3\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 2\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}2-3\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2-3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i} & 3 \\\\ -3 & 3\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3\\text{i}{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -3{x}_{1}+3\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(2-3\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables to be the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\text{i}{x}_{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=1,$ we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{i} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, $\\frac{a}{b}=\\frac{\\text{i}}{1}=\\text{i}.$"} :step/id #uuid "42de2582-1d6d-5484-ba6c-324ba0f7ff5f" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411586}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411586 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375432 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44822bc9-7e2e-562f-8058-69dd90f154f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375434} {:answer/id #uuid "a23d0c6f-90ba-502d-a418-481551cb71d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375435} {:answer/id #uuid "2c6d2e82-738d-5ce3-88ff-93e32f533bd2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375436} {:answer/id #uuid "696bc006-25c5-513a-bd9b-a6faa2dd9a36" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375437} {:answer/id #uuid "0ca94b17-5bb5-59b1-b428-73c7613972ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375438}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375438} :answer-field/id #uuid "16922444-299f-55a9-a2be-066c9885244c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375433}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "0ee811a8-c053-59bb-b00f-f224606c0564" :question/math-academy-id "q-81193" :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 8 \\\\ -2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-(-2)(8) & =0 \\\\ 1-2\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+16 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda +17 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{(-2)^{2}-4(1)(17)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =1\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =1\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=1+4\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 8 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}1+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i} & 8 \\\\ -2 & -4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-4\\text{i}{x}_{1}+8{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -2{x}_{1}-4\\text{i}{x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(1+4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{2}=\\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}{x}_{1}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\text{i}{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{1}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{1}=2,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ \\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=1-4\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}} & =\\frac{(2)(2)}{(\\text{i})(-\\text{i})}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "741564cd-c2f1-5aee-8b62-848c21e617d3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411588}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411587 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411584 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "006f21ef-bd9e-5319-853e-7e3fc38e4a67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411578} {:answer/id #uuid "4cc31227-522c-5e3c-865f-858998fd51d3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411579} {:answer/id #uuid "b38f6e8b-7681-58f7-b559-5a8bae4dff01" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411580} {:answer/id #uuid "c4dc0bd2-9334-5cf0-9566-65f6c6862a8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =-2\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411581} {:answer/id #uuid "fa45e365-566e-56f7-88cc-77bf8156b5ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =4\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411582}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411582} :answer-field/id #uuid "93c408a0-c9c5-54b6-81eb-ee9b40cb4448" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411583}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "db7154e6-6fda-5907-98f7-b3398371be54" :question/math-academy-id "q-38493" :question/problem "Determine if $v=[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors are non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -5 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8+4\\text{i} \\\\ 8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}8{\\text{i}}^{2}+4\\text{i} \\\\ 8\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}1+2\\text{i} \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =4\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=4\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =4\\text{i}.$"} :step/id #uuid "7c22f243-0739-5a0f-8281-eeed84387bab" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411589}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186272077 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "011ee86d-97e1-53d3-a3b6-63ede2abaf4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269268} {:answer/id #uuid "06c071d9-5963-5866-8063-d0604b2c6652" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{16}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186269329} {:answer/id #uuid "8a5b6a67-1956-5bd6-bf81-18c5c5717aec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270464} {:answer/id #uuid "9b8004b4-6cab-5417-9d66-a6e51002060e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{25}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186270640} {:answer/id #uuid "c4a5fd11-7f6b-58c9-94e4-6d1dc39b30af" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271007}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269268} :answer-field/id #uuid "21591163-e34d-5795-bc94-436e957ba0e4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186271570}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8845c35f-ffc0-56f1-b7af-e5bdef685e2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-140388" :question/problem "Given that ${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{1} \\\\ {b}_{1}\\end{aligned}]$ and ${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}{a}_{2} \\\\ {b}_{2}\\end{aligned}]$ are linearly independent eigenvectors of the matrix\n$A=[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}],$\nwhat is the value of $\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we need to find the eigenvalues of $A.$ To do that, we must solve the characteristic equation $det(A-\\lambda I)=0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}7-\\lambda  & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (7-\\lambda )(3-\\lambda )-(4)(-5) & =0 \\\\ 21-7\\lambda -3\\lambda +{\\lambda }^{2}+20 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-10\\lambda +41 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-10)\\pm \\sqrt{(-10)^{2}-4(1)(41)}}{2(1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-(-10)\\pm \\sqrt{-64}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{10\\pm 8\\text{i}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the eigenvalues are $\\lambda =5\\pm 4\\text{i}.$\n\nNow, let's consider the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1}=5+4\\text{i}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-{\\lambda }_{1}I & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & 4 \\\\ -5 & 3\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}5+4\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5+4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} & 4 \\\\ -5 & -2-4\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we get the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(2-4\\text{i}){x}_{1}+4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ -5{x}_{1}+(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since the matrix $A-(5+4\\text{i})I$ is singular, the system above must have a non-zero solution. This means that one equation of the system is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as the free variable. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\frac{1}{5}(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2}\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{5}(-2-4\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-5,$ we get the eigenvector\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2+4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n \n\nThen, to compute the second eigenvector corresponding to ${\\lambda }_{2}=5-4\\text{i}$, we simply compute the complex conjugate of ${\\overset{―}{v}}_{1},$ and get\n\n$$\n{v}_{2}={\\overset{―}{v}}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}2-4\\text{i} \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{b}_{1}{b}_{2}}{{a}_{1}{a}_{2}} & =\\frac{(-5)(-5)}{(2+4\\text{i})(2-4\\text{i})}=\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d4502c0c-e4ff-5fb4-9162-5b78360320c6"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411589 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186326744 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e967569-f453-574e-8919-a67fad95d2f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =13\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324758} {:answer/id #uuid "7b6f6a10-0bd5-5a15-8100-84cdb1b48c99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325009} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1224cc-38c3-56bc-8a03-7ccf158de176" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$v$ is not an eigenvector of $A$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325145} {:answer/id #uuid "ca079c89-eb27-58a1-8df2-a13f57b8062e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =-7\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325722} {:answer/id #uuid "e251a6af-588a-5940-924b-907ba00680c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\lambda =-13\\text{i}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325954}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325009} :answer-field/id #uuid "d985c21d-6faa-5e92-8ab4-fa9bd7e30639" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326615}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0ea0f7da-291a-5ef5-afec-bc983b4386ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-39181" :question/problem "Determine whether $v=[\\begin{aligned}-4+7\\text{i} \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}]$ is an eigenvector of the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 13 & 4\\end{aligned}].$\nIf so, then what is the corresponding eigenvalue?" :question/worked-solution "The eigenvectors of $A$ are the non-zero vectors $v$ such that $Av=\\lambda v.$ So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Av & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -5 \\\\ 13 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4+7\\text{i} \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-49-28\\text{i} \\\\ 91\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}49{\\text{i}}^{2}-28\\text{i} \\\\ 91\\text{i}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =7\\text{i}[\\begin{aligned}-4+7\\text{i} \\\\ 13\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =7\\text{i}v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $Av=7\\text{i}v,$ we conclude that $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ and the corresponding eigenvalue is $\\lambda =7\\text{i}.$"} :step/id #uuid "db758ac7-852d-51b4-ad30-e5acef4cfcd0" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186411585}}] :activity/title "Finding Complex Eigenvectors of Real 2x2 Matrices" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186411577}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176855} :activity/id #uuid "0b6911e4-5f07-57c1-b15a-445e4baf17de" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049091 :topic/id #uuid "d9b41e19-22fc-5fad-93ae-b8f50010d70b" :topic/math-academy-id 3557} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176855 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177064 :tutorial/content "Let's look again at the graph of the function $y=\\cos x.$ \n\n![](images/88/88378ece5b1721cdc533d07d69c004901539c7f8fda9a5a3e5c5648ed32905c5.png)\n\nThe cosine function has infinitely many zeros and an infinite number of points where the function reaches its maximum and minimum value. Let's write general expressions for these properties, assuming $n$ is an integer.\n\n**The Zeros of the Function**\n\nFrom the graph, we see that the zeros of the function are $x=\\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, \\pm \\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\dots$ \n\nA general expression for the zeros is\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{\\pi}{2} + n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Maxima of the Function**\n\nThe function has a maximum when $x=0$ and is periodic with period $2\\pi.$ Therefore, a general expression for the values of $x$ that maximize cosine is\n\n$$\nx = 2n\\pi.\n$$\n\n**The Minima of the Function**\n\nThe function has a minimum when $x=\\pi$ and is periodic with period $2\\pi.$ Therefore, a general expression for the values of $x$ that minimize cosine is\n\n$$\nx = \\pi + 2n\\pi.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "46e10740-fb99-59c2-8277-45bf39d4988e" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6896 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "96434733-6b0d-57f8-b32a-6d08028990e9" :step/math-academy-id 41780 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176856}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176856 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176047 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175693 :question/id #uuid "70569afd-42c4-5e5f-8397-4fbd873eb8e4" :question/math-academy-id "e-11002"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "542df339-5020-5680-a39c-a1ae161083e7" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050033} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050718}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176448} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176449} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186235452} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292696} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338799} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338800}] :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function"} :step/id #uuid "917ddcd7-bc4f-5e36-8f78-dd85b3ecbfe5" :step/math-academy-id 41781 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176857}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176857 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177065 :tutorial/content "Let's look at the graph of the cosine function again.\n\n![](images/04/04f13f2dec860874174f51990f4260615e7be893a6be5f86b3b13857c4e8953f.png)\n\nThe periodicity of the function is another characteristic of its graph. The period of the cosine function is $2\\pi,$ shown below:\n\n![](images/78/789fa06939a3e3350a18c4e9cdf078bf6baf3f025709703e608cb3b7ba89e698.png)\n\nThis means that $\\cos\\left( x+2n\\pi\\right) = \\cos \\left( x\\right)$ for any number $x$ and any integer $n.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "5cc2e21c-1a75-53dd-85be-61d9124f25c6" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6897 :tutorial/title "The Periodicity Property of the Cosine Function"} :step/id #uuid "95d7359c-3367-567b-8a0b-4546d0d324da" :step/math-academy-id 41784 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176858}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176858 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176048 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175694 :question/id #uuid "a8cc8b8f-463b-5734-a33d-e915159cba32" :question/math-academy-id "e-11003"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1c25c3a0-083a-55d5-b688-7347bfafaa9e" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049353}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176546} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176547} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237984} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338802} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338803}] :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Cosine Function"} :step/id #uuid "fdae8229-a567-5401-adbc-7dc3c17d7b91" :step/math-academy-id 41785 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176895}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176875 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176057 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175703 :question/id #uuid "08253dde-510a-5451-ba5c-64d42d647db2" :question/math-academy-id "e-11012"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "94645507-186a-54a7-a96b-ccfb0fb1f769" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049873}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176346} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176355} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186269129} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275428} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275430} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275434}] :knowledge-point/title "Utilizing the Evenness Property of the Cosine Function"} :step/id #uuid "763338f7-5348-5442-87d5-00b41679ebf8" :step/math-academy-id 41829} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176895 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186177082 :tutorial/content "Recall that a function $f(x)$ is *even* if it satisfies the property\n\n$$\nf(-x) = f(x).\n$$\n\nAs it turns out, cosine is an even function! So, for any value of $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\cos(-x) = \\cos(x).\n$$\n\nEven functions are always symmetric about the $y$-axis. This is indeed true of the cosine function, as we can see from the diagram below. \n\n![](images/b1/b1f44126ba6188d147026b6fe5a4066b0051ec3b638bb2d3e34ee1cbb1708cae.png)\n\nWe can use the evenness property of cosine in conjunction with its periodicity property to simplify trigonometric expressions. Let's see an example." :tutorial/id #uuid "c7ca8a70-b8fa-5e67-adec-ee263651a473" :tutorial/math-academy-id 7897 :tutorial/title "The Evenness Property of the Cosine Function"} :step/id #uuid "5a441a39-9db7-5be4-8b0d-dbbb978a8599" :step/math-academy-id 45521 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176875}}] :activity/title "Describing Properties of the Cosine Function" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176108}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176655} :activity/id #uuid "275b0afa-011e-599e-83db-08f12becae8b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049126 :topic/id #uuid "dcb0f682-2f5d-57d5-ac9a-10eb5e8a8b36" :topic/math-academy-id 3591} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176950 :tutorial/content "Given two finite data sets of equal length, say,\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}, \\qquad \ny=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\},\n$$\n\nthe **covariance** between $x$ and $y,$ denoted by $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y),$ is a measure that evaluates the extent to which $x$ and $y$ change together.\n\n- A positive covariance means that if $x$ increases, then $y$ is also likely to increase:\n\n![](images/bb/bbfae3f0fb38bc8652cd26716e4b0b128cf1f2039350267034d97161d7f5dac4.png)\n\n- A negative covariance means that if $x$ increases, then $y$ is likely to decrease:\n\n![](images/fd/fd45053fdc39f070e3438c3b8c61fe01613201595cfe00bd4cc6c6e481bf73ab.png)\n\n- A covariance of zero (or close to zero) indicates that there is no *linear* relationship between $x$ and $y.$\n\n![](images/a5/a58fa4d40d77af23df86b5bd8eaca3119b93bc9ea97cc435eb4e53ba2777b071.png)\n\n**Watch Out!** A covariance of zero does *not* mean that $x$ and $y$ are not related. It simply means the relationship between them is not linear. To illustrate, consider the image below.\n\n![](images/a4/a4bdbf92e691174644dda9b9a84acb1b896c0436afb221501dc10b4635841539.png)\n\nThe (nonlinear) relationship shown above is $y=x^2.$ However, it can be shown that $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = 0$ for this data set.\n\nTo calculate $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y),$ we use the following formula: \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y}),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{x}$  and $\\overline{y}$ denote the means of $x$ and $y$ respectively.\n\n**Note**: This formula for the covariance assumes that $x$ and $y$ are drawn from a *population*. There is a slightly different formula for covariance when $x$ and $y$ are sample data. However, we won't worry about the difference for now." :tutorial/id #uuid "da03b3e7-b452-59b9-b81e-5dd75cea02a2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10301 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "a9620c91-7f1f-5c90-81f9-15b63580b9b6" :step/math-academy-id 217080 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175936 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175746 :question/id #uuid "ef520918-4e4b-516b-acaa-57a14b5c1405" :question/math-academy-id "e-16134"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a2ac0065-9315-547a-a9cf-c6a5abea1aed" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050862}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176333} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268969} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268970} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268973} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268974} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268976} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275213} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275214} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275215}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Covariance"} :step/id #uuid "7c94f745-4162-5e7d-841a-0a7d3f00e84a" :step/math-academy-id 217081 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175937 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186175747 :question/id #uuid "33adfe2b-297e-5c52-8c4f-088d0c2b66e8" :question/math-academy-id "e-16136"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cee807c1-dd86-5b0e-a595-5c5754c47e4c" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268985} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268986} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268987} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268988} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268989} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268991} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275206} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275207} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275208} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275209} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275210}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing Covariance in Context"} :step/id #uuid "48e20fe8-234e-5200-b8a8-908f518d0da4" :step/math-academy-id 217084 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176658}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176951 :tutorial/content "Variance measures the spread of a *single* data set $x$ around its mean value $\\overline{x}.$ On the other hand, covariance measures the relationship between *two* data sets $x$ and $y.$\n\nThe (population) covariance of the data sets\n\n$$\nx=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}, \\qquad  \ny=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}\n$$\n\n \n\nis given by \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) =  \\dfrac{1}{n} \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i - \\overline{x})(y_i - \\overline{y}).\n$$\n\nIf we compare a single data set, say $x$, against itself using our formula for covariance, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Cov}(x,x) & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & ={\\sigma }^{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\sigma^2$ denotes the variance of $x.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "42b9ca98-be0b-50e0-9b90-f3e38732fcd0" :tutorial/math-academy-id 10305 :tutorial/title "The Connection Between Variance and Covariance"} :step/id #uuid "2661268b-e076-5805-af10-02ffd1c3fc5e" :step/math-academy-id 217087}] :activity/title "Covariance" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186176135}] [{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163004} :activity/id #uuid "c9745592-4ec7-557a-9119-d1dcdcf23d32" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049149 :topic/id #uuid "de945224-de03-5292-84ca-39d9ddf3f7e4" :topic/math-academy-id 3003} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163004 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163288 :tutorial/content "Let's recall the multiplication law for conditional probability for two events ![](images/32/3207697e43e4f88223ad85c16964c3103a50c0c999a4ca14417695158d45526b.svg) and ![](images/5d/5d8b650b90c85fbc0db5e4b6fae61767e293a843d18a2526f3ee4157733e1f4b.svg) ![](images/44/44229a2a35108e36c1348009a552be50e798af9b2807f49c4e4ef8515ed7eefe.svg)\n\nNow suppose that ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/ab/ab4817775494786b06f0b9cecf225ea5979c035b69c36c1fa078f835524be9e9.svg) are discrete random variables. The conditional probability that ![](images/2c/2cc57b1c8b00862c078193ea1774f4012e1513ab1a26a6d5bb806e046220a812.svg) *given that* ![](images/46/46851627a248368806191a89ed2bb59d72b83b2e352f768da26f41305e156052.svg) follows immediately from the multiplication law and is given by\n\n![](images/2b/2b705d0acedbb080625148287647d075dce75f40531615a52352a848a85892fe.svg)\n\nwhere ![](images/a2/a2dcac06a4cf671df0fa43cef1727449fb2ebc57fb824d19cc07da5e75d12176.svg) is the joint PMF of ![](images/46/46833dd7644e5c60bb4525fe4e7649a99752b26096331755a4b44c2abe183f11.svg) and ![](images/07/07fd689b67975f50da1b6b025460996fc87f21f758f5e98d34eb84c9b0e50fcd.svg) and ![](images/3a/3aa9c47fc054317f2163393853c05d71714e0c9f